@page "/exchart"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="Overview"
          Lead="Line, step and point charts of the data your application shows, drawn as SVG by Blazor. Hand ExChart a definition in your data's terms; it draws each series as what it is, and refuses rather than draws something that only looks right.">

    <DocsSection Title="A first chart">
        <p>
            A <code>ChartDefinition</code> is an axis and its series. Each series has an id, a name, a
            Series Kind and its points; hand the definition to <code>ExChart</code> with a size. Move over the
            plot to read every series at that date, click a legend entry to hide it, and drag across the plot
            to narrow the range. Under MudBlazor, <code>MudChartChrome.Default</code> draws the toolbar and the
            context menu with MudBlazor's controls, and the theme's Primary, Secondary and Tertiary colour the
            first three series.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FirstChart)" Mud="typeof(FirstChartMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/ChartData.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
        <p>
            <strong>In React.</strong> <code>@@ex-grid/chart-react</code>'s <code>ExChart</code> (published with 0.3.0)
            takes a <code>chrome</code> of two render functions, as <code>IChartChrome</code> has two parts:
            <code>toolbar</code>, handed the toolbar's commands and, while it is open, Chart Filters, and
            <code>menu</code>, handed the context menu's commands. Each is drawn through a portal where the built-in
            markup stands, and is handed a new context only when what it shows changed, so the text typed in Chart
            Filters is kept until Apply. The commands, their order, what a typed range reads as and where the menu
            opens stay the chart's: invoke a command, hand Chart Filters' <code>apply</code> the text, and the chart
            says what it could not read.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The parts of a chart">
        <p>
            These pages and the API name the parts of a chart as the lists under the figures do. The toolbar
            opens Chart Filters, shows the chart as a table and saves its files; the legend hides a series. Move
            over the plot and the tooltip lists every series of that panel at the pointer's position, while the
            guide runs down every panel. A value axis that does not reach zero keeps a break mark at its foot,
            and what the chart did to fit, such as candles merged into weeks, is said in the Notes under it.
        </p>
        <DocsFigure Name="chart-anatomy">
            Two years of an index as weekly candles with a 25-day average and the volume in a panel below, the
            pointer resting on the plot. A script draws the figure from
            <a href="figure/chart-anatomy" target="_blank">the chart on a page of its own</a>.
        </DocsFigure>
        <p>
            Under MudBlazor, the Chart Dock holds the charts made from a grid's Selection, a tab each, beside
            or below the grid and never over it. The grid's context menu makes a new chart, its series in
            columns or in rows, or adds the Selection's series to a chart already open.
        </p>
        <DocsFigure Name="chart-dock-anatomy">
            A curve history selected and charted, with the context menu open over the Selection again. A script
            draws the figure from <a href="figure/chart-dock-anatomy" target="_blank">the grid and its Chart Dock on a page of their own</a>.
        </DocsFigure>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Two ways to make a chart">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>On its own</strong>, from a definition the page builds, as above.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>From a grid's selection</strong>: the grid's context menu charts the selected cells,
                and the chart keeps following the rows it was made from. See
                <a href="exchart/grid">From the grid</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What ExChart does, and what it leaves to you">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Every series says what it is.</strong> A level is joined by a line, a rate over an
                interval is a step, an amount per period is drawn per year. A series without a Series Kind is
                refused, not drawn with a default. See <a href="exchart/series">Series and axes</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It draws only what it is given.</strong> No spline, no extrapolation, no interpolation:
                a missing value is a gap, never a zero, and a curve between quotes is your analytics', declared
                as such.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It holds no View State.</strong> The hidden series, the range and the axis come back as
                events, for the page to keep, store or hand back. See
                <a href="exchart/tooltip">Tooltip and Chart Filters</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It is read aloud.</strong> Each chart has a summary for a screen reader, and "View as
                table" shows the values it draws. Its CSV, SVG and PNG are saved on one click. See
                <a href="exchart/export">Reading and export</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Nothing per point reaches JavaScript.</strong> The plot is SVG elements in components that
                skip their render; a pointer move redraws the tooltip and the guide, not the series.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A chart drawn in the browser">
        <p>
            Blazor's ExChart is moving onto the same TypeScript core as React's, as ExGrid and ExPivot are.
            <code>ExChart.Script.ExChart</code> and <code>ExChart.Script.ExSurface</code> are that chart and that
            surface, beside the ones above while the move is under way: the drawing, the tooltip, the legend, Chart
            Filters, View as table and the turning of a surface run in the browser on both hosting models, so on Blazor
            Server a pointer moved over the plot no longer crosses the circuit. They take the same
            <code>ChartDefinition</code> and <code>SurfaceDefinition</code>, the same parameters and the same events,
            and a click hands back the Row Key the point was handed in with.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreChart)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/ChartData.cs"])" />
        <p>
            A .NET function cannot run in the browser. A series' or a surface's <code>Format</code> crosses as the
            text it writes each value the definition holds, so a value reads as your Format writes it; a decimal
            written <code>3.30</code> stays <code>3.30</code> in the tooltip and the table. Where the chart would ask
            a function for a value the definition does not hold, it refuses the definition with a
            <code>NotSupportedException</code> that names what to change, rather than write that value some other
            way: an axis <code>Format</code>, which writes every tick; a series made by
            <code>ChartSeries.Sampled</code>, which is asked a value at every pixel; a <code>Format</code> on
            candles, which writes their change and a merged candle's volume; a <code>Format</code> on a surface axis
            placed by value or on a mesh's axis, which writes the ticks between its values, unless it writes them as
            plainly as a sampled surface's axis does; and a <code>ZFormat</code> on a mesh, whose slice reads values
            between its nodes. Two Row Keys, or two values of a surface axis, that would reach the browser as one
            value, such as <code>1</code> and <code>1.0m</code>, are refused too. A Chart Spec, a Chrome and the
            grid's Chart menu are not carried yet: use <code>ExChart.Components.ExChart</code> for those. A candle's
            values, and every value in its CSV, are written as numbers, <code>3.3</code>, where the Razor
            component keeps a decimal's <code>3.30</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="WebAssembly, and AOT">
        <p>
            AOT is not required. Measured on WebAssembly, ahead-of-time compilation redraws a chart about 30%
            faster, while hiding a series is no faster, and the application's download grows from 2.9 MB to
            22 MB. A chart redraws within a frame without it; turn it on only when a measurement of your own
            pages says so.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
